st remains  the preferred compendium  pass fail test method and backed up by  the microscopic  membrane method where required this  revised chapter introduces  for the first time the orthogonal or  complementary  flow imaging test method as a useful  complementary tool to these methods and  it emphasizes its use for characterizing  the particle load  in in products and how it can help with  forensic examinations  to identify the contaminating particles  to support  product improvement of course as well as  root cause investigations  so here's today's agenda  today we'll take an overview of the  revised united states pharmacopoeia  chapter  178 and then we'll move on to take a  look at the compendium pass fail test  for subvisible particles in injectable  drugs the hayak  light obscuration test  there are multiple usp chapters on  subvisible particles in parenteral  products  and we'll discuss the calibration and  measurement range is covered by this  chapter  we'll take a look at some of the  examples of particles you may find in  your products  and then we'll take a look at the  complementary orthogonal  culture counter and laser diffraction  methods  as called out in the revised  complementary chapter usp1787  so first let's take an overview of the  revised 1788  chapter  the revised one 788 became official  on first of may of this year 2021 and  178  is of course um in the 1000 chapters so  it's  it's a name for informational chapter  compilating complementing the chapters  787 788 789 and of course  as i mentioned or already one seven  eight seven  chapter 788 is harmonized with the  european  and japanese pharmacopoeia chapters  on subvisible particle matter and  injection injections  where the hayak light obscuration method  remains  the official pass fail technique for  particulate matter  but for the first time 1788 introduces  the idea or concept of flow imaging  there's a complementary orthogonal test  to the hyac light obscuration method  and that's the compendial test fail  method but the the flow imaging  um is a useful complementary tool it  provides all focal information  to help characterize the particle  loading products and  it can also be used to help with the  forensic examination to identify  contaminating particles  to support product improvement as well  as root cause investigations when you  have a fail  of the hayek light observation test  1788 encourages users to develop a  strategy  of particle determination in order to  identify the category  if not the identity of the particle  population  so is to build a comprehensive control  strategy  for final production  so now let's take a look at that  compendium pass fail test them  mandated in usp the hayek light  obscuration  technique  the hayak light obscuration test test  method  was introduced to the usb in 1985  and it remains the compendium pass fail  testing method  the hayak method normalizes irregular  shaped  particles into equivalent  diameter results as if they were  circular particles  and this eliminates the human error  issue with microscopic membrane particle  counting  excuse me a laser light shines through a  measurement view volume  and lands on a detector which turns the  light energy  into an electrical signal  the sample under test is drawn through  the measurement view volume  as particles in the sample pass through  the measurement view volume  they block or obscure a portion of the  laser light  and this reduces the light falling on  the detector  and causes the detector electrical  output signal to drop proportionally  by comparing the size of the drop in the  electrical signal to that caused by  calibration beads which is certified and  traceable  the hayak can now report an equivalent  circular diameter  to that particle no matter what shape  that particle is  this allows the hi-hat to be calibrated  with traceable certified beads of known  sizes and to standardize this test test  method  across the industry  as 1788 supports the chapters 787  788 789 it gives guidance on the  recommended sizes of calibration  beads used to calibrate the hayak  counter  although the compendium pass fail tests  in 787  and 788 reference limits for the number  of particles at just  two size thresholds greater or equal to  10 microns  and greater or equal to 25 microns  this revised 1788 chapter suggests there  is value  in evaluating the particulate load in  the sub 10 micron range  and recommends that routine testing  using the higher  light obscuration method be extended to  investigate particle loads down to 2  microns  it's just adding measurements to greater  than equal  to 2 micron and greater than equal to  5 microns are also very useful  and it points out that the measurements  two microns are really  a great indication of the quantity of  protein  aggregates in a biologic  as the chapter for opthalmics 789  also requires particle sizing and  counters counting sorry  at greater or equal to 50 micron it also  suggests that data in the greater than  100 micron range  is useful too so it goes on to suggest  the calibration of the hayak using  tracer traceable spherical speeds  2 5 10 15 25  and 15 microns in size  so on to the recommended library of  particle  data as can be seen by these images from  a scanning  electron microscope particulates are  very rarely  uniformly shaped they're not spherical  1788 recommends a strategy of a holistic  use of a kayak  and orthogonal test methods right from  drug development  and continuing to final product qc in  production  in order to build a library of reference  particle data  to help technicians with particle  classification  risk assessment and even identification  of these particle substances  now the revised 1788 chapter refers to  the complementary  chapter which has also been revised 1787  were made official on the 1st of may  this year 2021.  now 787 recommends that the coulter  counter  and laser diffraction are regularly used  alongside the high  light obscuration test to gain more  information  on particle size and distribution so  let's take a look  at the culture cancer counter principle  although flow imaging and microscope  method are recommended as  orthogonal test methods to the higher  light obscuration method  the revised 1788 chapter is clear  that reported particle sizes will vary  between these methods  due to the different measurement  technologies used  in each case the imperfect well  certainly in light  light obscuration and laser diffraction  the imperfect method of using  two-dimensional information to describe  a three-dimensional object  it's bound to lead to variable results  being reported  with further complications arising in  samples contain  heterogeneous mixtures of particles  i.e a population of varying particle  sizes  now in the culture counter however the  particle  three-dimensional size is measured in  terms of the volume of the particle  a voltage is applied across two  electrodes immersed in a conducting  solution  such as a buffer solution typically used  for  protein therapies the electrodes are  separated by a precision aperture  the sample test is drawn through the  precision aperture between these  electrodes  and particles present in the sample will  partially block  the aperture as they pass through and  they displace a  volume of the conducting solution  in response the culture increases the  applied voltage between the electrodes  to maintain a constant current through  that aperture  and the size of the increase in the  voltage is directly proportional to the  volume of the particle that's  partially blocking the aperture the  culture counter provides not  only the equivalent spherical diameter  but also particle volume and as such the  culture is  very sensitive to small variations in  particle radius because of course  volume measurement represents the cube  of the particle radius  ie volume is four thirds pi  r cubed so any small changes in the  radius are multiplied by a  cube law making them easier to detect  as a volume change  now coming to our last topic today  we'll take a look at our laser  diffraction the third complementary  method described in 1787 alongside  hayek and coulter  now laser diffraction is a  ensemble technique so it measures a  population of particles all at the same  time instead of the other two which is  one at a time  so it gives a distribution of the  particles present in the samples under  test  now one weakness with laser diffraction  used by itself  is that smaller particles and a mixed  tetragenous  particle population of particles sizes  can remain undetected because the bigger  particles  create so much light uh diffraction  so to overcome this the weaknesses lace  diffraction can be combined  with polarized intensity differential  scattering system  which provides particle sizing  distribution information  for those samples containing  heterogeneous particle populations  and this allows for the detection of sub  1 micron particles  in the presence of particles in the 10  to 100 micron  range  so in conclusion the revised chapter  1788  encourages users to develop a  comprehensive strategy of particle  determination  in order to identify the category as i  said if not the identity  of the particle population so as to  build a compound  comprehensive control strategy  the hyac light obscuration test remains  the compendium  pass fail test method and culture  laser diffraction and flow imaging are  recommended as orthogonal complementary  methods  so that brings me to the end of my very  short presentation today i know it's  very high level overview but  i'd like now to open the floor for  questions  thank you tony for that informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have any questions you'd like to  ask please do so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen and we'll answer as  many questions as we have time for  so let's take a look it seems we already  have some great questions coming in  tony our first audience member says  thank you for your presentation please  can you explain the difference provided  by the results between the hayak method  and the culture and laser  diffraction methods  thank you no thank you very much for  that question it's a good question  so as we said the hayak remains the  compendium or pass fail test so this is  the one  you're about using your batch release  testing on and it's used for  product release as i said in usp ep and  jp  it does eliminate the human error in  using a manual microscope method which  is superseded to size particles  but it only reports equivalent  two-dimensional particle matter diameter  sorry  the use of this instrument is harmonized  as i mentioned across  usb ep and jp so  the equivalency between the reported  pharmacopoeial test results has been  established between these two  test methods however the culture counter  on the other hand  measures the volume of the particles and  calculates  the equivalent 3d spherical diameter  and this makes it much more sensitive to  small variations  in the particle radius however its  measurement range is limited depending  on the aperture size chosen for the  measurement  so multiple measurements are typically  required  when you have a mixed particle size  population  it also reports particle counts just  like the hayak but the laser diffraction  method on the other hand  is a dynamic particle sizing technique  that can measure  and report particle size distribution  but not counts  in a sample containing particles from 10  nanometers up to three and a half  thousand microns  just from one measurement but i'll say  it does not report particle count data  just distribution  thank you so much tony now if the  compendial pass fail  test particle sizes are 10 and 25  microns  why is the revised usp1788  recommending the hayak is calibrated  from 2 microns up to 50 microns  so what the usb 78  1788 is trying to encourage the  development  of a particle sizing and classification  identification  strategy not just a pass fail compendium  test  so based on knowledge from the  orthogonal testing of particle  populations  in formulations users can build a  picture  of trends within the product population  populations that  may indicate product is trending out of  compliance  before it fails the kayak test  and this warrants further investigation  as i mentioned several times the height  remains the compendium gatekeeper if you  like for  pass fail testing but if you calibrate  and configure it  to report particle concentrations from  two microns  up to 50 microns and above it can give  early warning  of a potentially out of compliance trend  in product quality in manufacturing  and this will prompt further orthogonal  testing to get to root cause  before the product goes out of  compliance  thank you so much tony we have one more  question coming in from our audience  can you please explain why the colored  light scattering  is required to enable laser diffraction  method to accurately report a particle  load of mixed  sizes of course of course  in order to measure the small particles  particularly in a  population of both small and large  particles  you need to capture the side scattering  data from a polarized color  coloured light source and that's what  the polarized  system does this data is fed into the  calculation from the laser diffraction  of the particle population and it allows  the instrument to  know there are smaller particles there  and to accurately report particles  less than one micron in the size of a  mixed population of much larger  particles otherwise you wouldn't see  them  tony thank you so much for your  presentation today would you like to  provide  any final comments for our audience  before we go  thank you yeah i think it's this this  chapter is really interesting because it  changes  um the the view of uh  hayak testing um from just  final qc testing for the compendium pass  fail con  test right through to pushing it  upstream  into the drug development and process  development  space so the hike now should be used all  the way through  and as i said in the presentation  names culture as the as a good  orthogonal test  for more information about those  particles and  introduces laser diffraction uh  which is good for the research and drug  development process development stage  but  also um it suggests that these  culture and lace diffraction can be used  in the gmp space gmp manufacturing space  for particulate analysis as an  orthogonal test  to the hayak in the qc testing  tony i want to thank you again for your  time today and for your important  research  and i also want to thank lab roots and  our sponsor beckman coulter life  scientists for underwriting today's  educational webcast  and before we go i want to thank the  audience for joining us today  and for their interesting questions  questions we did not have  time for today and those submitted  during the on-demand period  will be addressed by our speaker via the  contact information  you provided at the time of registration  and this webcast  can be viewed on demand lab routes will  alert you via email when it's available  for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's  live event until next time take care  everyone  bye


